{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
  "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen Sanders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SANDERS: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: If a prosecutor's absolute immunity in judicial proceedings means anything, it means that a prosecutor may not be sued because a trial has ended in a conviction, Yet that is exactly what happened in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Your -- your case here is a polite way of telling us we wasted our time in Buckley v. Fitzsimmons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen Sanders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SANDERS: Your Honor, I don't believe so at all. I think that this case presents exactly the question that Buckley reserved, and that is whether the fabrication of evidence by a prosecutor in and of itself, without regard to its use in some way, states a constitutional cause of action."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Does that mean that, even if we were dealing with police officers who did what the prosecutors were alleged to have done at the investigation stage, no prosecutor, only police investigators, the fact that a trial and a conviction had occurred would mean that the police officers were not liable, either?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You're not answering the question clearly. Are both the prosecutor, in Justice Ginsburg's hypothetical, and the policeman liable? Can't you answer that? Yes or no."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Why do we need that theory? I mean, why not just say what Newman said and the others said? There is no free-standing right."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: What if a prosecutor knows\nthat it's fabricated evidence? The police officer\nfabricates the evidence and says: Mr. Prosecutor, it's\na very bad man; I fabricated the evidence. The\nprosecutor introduces it. What result there?\nSee, your footnote 6 presumes that the\nprosecutor doesn't know."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KATYAL: And if the prosecutor does\nknow, we don't think that there is a Fifth Amendment due\nprocess violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: A prosecutor is not\ngoing to flinch when he suspects evidence is perjured or\nfabricated? Do you really want to send a message to\npolice officers that they should not merely flinch but\nstop if they have reason to believe that evidence is\nfabricated?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What would you have to\nallege to get by Iqbal or to get by summary judgment?\nYou allege that the testimony at trial was false and\nthat the prosecutor knew that it was false."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Before the re-interview,\nbecause the prosecutor doesn't want to take the case to\nthe grand jury before looking this witness in the eye\nand seeing whether this -- this guy who's got a lot of\nimpeachment baggage is -- is a -- is a credible witness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You will have to refresh my memory. Wasn't that a Fourth Amendment violation ultimately?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The lower court in Malley actually accepted exactly this argument, that if you have any absolutely immune act in the causal chain that breaks it off. This Court rejected it and was frankly fairly dismissive, dismissed it in the footnote, footnote 7 of the opinion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0002-0002",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the consequences of accepting their view is to really turn all of your absolute immunity cases into a fool's errand."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen Sanders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We have four main points.\nBeginning with the Second Circuit's decision\nin Zahrey and subsequent cases in the Second Circuit,\nhave significantly cut back on the meaning of Zahrey.\nThe Wray decision, which we discussed in our reply\nbrief, the Gonzalez decision, which we discussed in our\nopening brief, have not allowed for this kind of\ncontinuous liability for a prosecutor.\nThey have made very clear, particularly the\nGonzalez decision, that when it is a prosecutor's\nactions before a judge advocating on behalf of the\nState, that are responsible for a deprivation of\nliberty, in that situation, absolute immunity applies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What -- what does that\nprove? What does that prove? I don't understand why\nyou bring that up because it shows that the fact that\nthere aren't many cases, only 17 in the -- in the Second\nCircuit, it doesn't mean anything because the Second\nCircuit is not applying as liberal a rule as your\nopponent suggests."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen Sanders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SANDERS: No, Your Honor. I think it's\nto -- I think it's to say that the Zahrey decision has\nnot had the kind of impact and has not been applied in a\nway that respondents are asking for it to be applied."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen Sanders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0003-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It would work a radical change in the law of\nimmunity because it would mean that far more wrongful\nconviction claims against prosecutors would go forward\nunder only qualified immunity.\nThat is the inevitable consequence of\naffirming the courts below in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7c788d6409ac-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Yes, but what about the\npretrial detention? Isn't that a deprivation of\nliberty?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1065",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "7c788d6409ace4ce6ac62ea666b9ad10b9471b2715e57d3f14a483c35681072b",
